That works if mock_devices_plugin.git is a bare Git repository on your Mac. Git push/pull transfers committed Git history and files—no rsync exclusions are needed.

1. On your Mac, verify it’s bare and check your branch:

git --git-dir=/Users/javierrefuerzo/development/plugins/mock_devices_plugin.git rev-parse --is-bare-repository

cd /Users/javierrefuerzo/development/plugins/plugin_mock_devices
git branch --show-current
git status


The first command should print true. Commit the changes you want to transfer; git push won’t include uncommitted changes. Be sure not to add credentials or local-only files.

2. Add the bare repo as a remote and push from your project checkout:

git remote add macrepo /Users/javierrefuerzo/development/plugins/mock_devices_plugin.git
git push macrepo HEAD


If macrepo is already configured, use git remote set-url macrepo /Users/javierrefuerzo/development/plugins/mock_devices_plugin.git instead. Note the branch name printed by git branch --show-current.

3. On the controller, pull from your Mac over SSH. First enable Remote Login on your Mac and make sure the controller can reach it. Then, on the controller, if ~/projects/mock_devices is already a Git checkout:

cd ~/projects/mock_devices
git remote add macrepo ssh://javierrefuerzo@MAC_IP/Users/javierrefuerzo/development/plugins/mock_devices_plugin.git
git fetch macrepo
git pull --ff-only macrepo BRANCH


Replace MAC_IP with your Mac’s LAN IP and BRANCH with the branch name from step 1. If the macrepo remote already exists in that checkout, update it with git remote set-url macrepo ... rather than adding it again.

